+- [ ] **C-13 Hatches, lineweights and plot styles** — Screen and exported output agree on declared styles. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Hatching is genuine scanline geometry with ring containment and hole exclusion across five patterns, and per-primitive widths are emitted for plan, elevation and section output (`src/studio/architect/drawing.ts` hatchLines). Remaining: no user control of lineweight, colour or plot style, and no proof that screen and exported PDF agree on declared styles. [section 02]
+- [ ] **C-14 Undo, redo and transaction history** — One user operation undoes atomically without losing active context. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Undo and redo form a two-stack history capped at 60 steps with redo invalidated on a new edit, bound to Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y, and one architect operation is one transaction (`src/studio/architect/ArchitectWorkspace.tsx`). The assistant undo path is separately proven in the SC-08 journeys. Remaining: history is cleared on project load, so undo does not survive a reopen, and no inspected screenshot covers the keyboard path. [section 02]
 
 ### D — Documents, sheets and drawing registers
 
 Benchmark references: [Autodesk AutoCAD](https://www.autodesk.com/products/autocad), [Bluebeam markups and data](https://www.bluebeam.com/product/markups-and-data/), [Oracle Aconex](https://www.oracle.com/construction-engineering/aconex/). These references inform the category; each acceptance requirement is X-Ray's own target.
 
-- [ ] **D-01 Multi-file and multi-page import** — PDFs with different page sizes preserve every original page. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Multi-page PDF import stores each page with its own identity and dimensions, and a 13-page Redburn set was imported and retained through native runs (SC-10, SC-13). Remaining: a fixture with deliberately mixed page sizes has not been asserted page by page.
-- [ ] **D-02 Sheet names and numbers** — Rename changes labels without breaking evidence references. State: partial — verified in development 2026-09-08 for source sheets: rename changes only the page label/discipline; calibrations, traces, items, annotations and saved views stay keyed by original page index and byte-identical; the new name shows in the register, heading and navigation after reload. Fixture Redburn BR250157 (SHA-256 b57956f76b5dc893ac2b28a021f3f92e345807e326d6b1f8313e373f9145ad38, 13 pages); 37 focused tests; three verifiers not refuted. Code/proof: [SC-03 sheets](proof/growth/2026-09-08-az3-sheets/README.md). Remaining: authored architectural sheets, production/native build.
-- [ ] **D-03 Add and duplicate drawing sheets** — New sheet receives an independent identity and layout. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Authored sheets can be added, duplicated, renamed, reordered, archived and recovered, each with an independent identity and layout, and duplication does not share viewport records (`src/studio/architect/authoredSheetSet.ts`, tests pass 2026-09-10). Remaining: no inspected screenshot of the authored sheet lifecycle at the declared viewports.
-- [ ] **D-04 Remove with impact preview** — Review lists dependent takeoffs, links and viewports before removal. State: partial — verified in development 2026-09-08 for source-page archive (the product's removal): the review lists calibrations, measured traces, located items, annotations, linked photos and, newly, saved views for that page, and storage stays unchanged until confirmation. Code/proof: [SC-03 sheets](proof/growth/2026-09-08-az3-sheets/README.md). Remaining: whole-source removal, SHA-bound construction takeoff registers (not page-scoped, so not counted), production/native build.
-- [ ] **D-05 Restore removed sheets** — Restored sheet recovers annotations, scale and original ordering. State: partial — verified in development 2026-09-08 as archive → recover: the page returns to its original managed slot with identical metadata, its locked scale and a real sketch annotation byte-identical after recover and reload (unit test with job evidence after archive and recover; browser assertions on 13 calibrations and the annotation at every step). Code/proof: [SC-03 sheets](proof/growth/2026-09-08-az3-sheets/README.md). Remaining: authored sheets, production/native build.
-- [ ] **D-06 Reorder and group sheets** — Discipline groups and order persist after restart and export. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Verified in the 2026-09-07 acceptance addendum in this file for source-sheet discipline groups, persistent managed order and an actual ordered-register download, retaining every original page identity through reload in browser and Windows-native runs. Remaining: the export is a JSON register, and authored multi-sheet publishing and reordering the source PDF itself are outside that acceptance.
-- [ ] **D-07 Title blocks and templates** — Project metadata updates linked fields without clipping. State: partial (re-assessed 2026-09-11). Corrected from gap: the 2026-09-10 assessment stated that project metadata cannot update linked fields, and that is wrong. A title block is drawn on every authored sheet and its fields are already linked to project metadata: project name, address, sheet number, design revision, paper size, model revision, a rotating north arrow driven by the sheet's northAngle, and a scale bar computed from the sheet scale (`src/studio/architect/ArchitectSheets.tsx`, the title strip below the frame; `authoredSheetSet.ts` holds number and northAngle per sheet; 21/21 architect tests pass 2026-09-11). Remaining, and the reason this is not verified: the block is hardcoded at fixed coordinates with no template concept, so its content and layout cannot be chosen, no firm or client title block can be substituted, and there is the fit is now proven at the maximum stored lengths (name 200, address 500) both on screen and in the exported PDF. The exported drawing is fitted with the embedded font's own metrics: at the schema maximum a 500 character address is drawn as 213 characters with a truncation marker on A3 and in full on A1, read back from the PDF content stream rather than asserted (proof/growth/2026-09-11-d13-export/README.md, pdf-text-verification.log; 51/51 tests). Remaining: no template system, so the block's content and layout cannot be chosen and no firm or client title block can be substituted; and no inspected screenshot of the PDF itself, because Chrome's PDF plugin does not expose its render surface to CDP here.
-- [ ] **D-08 Viewports and per-view scale** — Independent scales measure correctly in exported PDF. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). A sheet carries up to twelve viewports with independent view, level, scale and position, and resizing the paper refits frames without altering viewport scales (`src/studio/architect/authoredSheetSet.ts`). Export is real vector PDF (`sheets.ts`). Remaining: measuring an exported PDF at each declared viewport scale has not been executed.
-- [ ] **D-09 Drawing revisions and supersession** — Old revision remains retrievable and visibly superseded. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-12). Implemented: issue sets record immutable historical issue records and frozen sheet layouts with deterministic SHA-256 layout hashing and design revision tracking (`src/studio/architect/issueHistory.ts`, `model.ts`, `ArchitectSheets.tsx`). When a new revision is issued (e.g. Rev B), prior current issues automatically transition to `superseded` status with timestamps and pointers to the successor revision (`supersededByRevision: "B"`). The Drawing sheets tab displays an expandable "Drawing revisions & history" control with `CURRENT` and `SUPERSEDED` status badges and an issue register table. Clicking "Inspect layout" on an older revision retrieves the frozen layout onto the canvas stage, with a prominent `.arch-superseded-banner` alert and a diagonal semi-transparent red `SUPERSEDED` SVG watermark across the paper drawing, while locking viewport and sheet editing controls. Proven by 8 unit tests covering issue recording, stale-review refusal, supersession transitions, retrievability of modified/deleted sheets, and chronological lineage (92/92 architect tests passing, `tsc --noEmit` exit 0), plus an inspected Fast CDP browser scenario (`proof/growth/2026-09-12-d09-revisions/README.md`, `fast-cdp-execution.log`, 3 screenshots at 1680×1050). Remaining, and why this is not verified: development browser only, with no production minification build, no installed desktop package run, and no tablet capture; revision lineage is single-sequence rather than multi-party branching; and historical issues are persisted in client project state / LocalStorage rather than a central cloud-synced revision vault.
-- [ ] **D-10 Revision overlay and slip-sheeting** — Added/deleted geometry is inspectable with alignment controls. State: gap (assessed 2026-09-10). There is no revision overlay or slip-sheeting, so added and deleted geometry cannot be inspected between issues.
-- [ ] **D-11 OCR and sheet indexing** — Suggested names are reviewable and uncertainty is visible. State: gap (assessed 2026-09-10). There is no OCR or automatic sheet indexing, and therefore no suggested names or visible uncertainty.
-- [ ] **D-12 Cross-sheet references and hyperlinks** — Detail callout opens the intended sheet revision. State: gap (assessed 2026-09-10). Cross-sheet references and detail callouts are not implemented.
-- [ ] **D-13 Batch printing and issue sets** — Selected sheets export in reviewed order with an issue register. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-12). Implemented: selected sheets are issued as one PDF whose first page is a drawing issue register listing every sheet in printed order with its number, size, scale and viewport count, plus the issue purpose, design revision, model revision and timestamp. Order follows the design's managed sheet order rather than click order, so the issued set matches the register the user reads on screen. An issue is reviewed before export and the review is refused if the design changed after it, in the UI and again inside the export function so a programmatic caller cannot bypass it. Each sheet renders through the existing single-sheet path and the pages are merged, so an issued drawing is the drawing the user saw, and mixed A1/A3 paper sizes survive (`src/studio/architect/issueSet.ts`, `sheets.ts` exportIssueSetPdf, `ArchitectSheets.tsx`). Proven by reading a committed 5-page issue back from its own bytes and by an inspected desktop browser journey covering selection, review and the stale-review refusal; 73/73 tests, `tsc --noEmit` exit 0 (proof/growth/2026-09-12-d13-issue-set/README.md). Remaining, and why this is not verified: development-server browser only, with no built-browser or installed-app run and no tablet capture; the browser proof does not exercise the file download itself; and no issue history is stored, so the project holds no record of what was issued when, which D-09 supersession would need. Single-sheet PDF export remains assessed under D-08.
-- [ ] **D-14 Bookmarks and saved views** — Personal navigation restores the correct page and viewport. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Verified in the 2026-09-07 acceptance addendum in this file for local original-page, zoom and source-relative-centre bookmarks, with pan, zoom, page switching, viewport restoration, resize, reload, removal and failed-write preservation passing, and a tablet sub-check at both declared orientations. Remaining: saved views are local project metadata, with no account synchronisation or sharing.
-- [ ] **D-15 Visual and vector revision delta** — Two issued revisions report added, removed and changed geometry with a quantity variance table. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-12). Implemented: pure revision delta comparison engine (`src/studio/architect/revisionDelta.ts`) and project geometry snapshot capture on drawing issue (`src/studio/architect/issueHistory.ts`, `model.ts`). When an issue set is exported, a frozen geometry snapshot of project levels, walls, hosted openings, slabs, roofs, and annotations is saved alongside the sheet layouts. In the Drawing sheets tab (`src/studio/architect/ArchitectSheets.tsx`), an interactive "Compare revisions (Revision Delta)" panel enables instant comparison between any two issued revisions (e.g. Rev A vs Rev B) or between an issue and live WIP design. Reports: (1) high-level KPI cards for sheet modifications, geometry changes, net area variance, and material cost delta; (2) color-coded delta cards for added, removed, and modified sheets (detecting size, scale, and layout hash differences); (3) vector geometry element delta cards tracking modified wall lengths/alignments/layers, hosted door/window parameters (sill, hinge, swing, dimensions), slabs, and roofs; and (4) an interactive Quantity Variance Schedule with trade/material breakdowns, unit of measurement, baseline vs target quantities, delta (Δ) with color-coded badges, and percentage change. Proven by 7 unit tests (99/99 architect tests passing, `tsc --noEmit` exit 0) and an inspected Fast CDP browser scenario (`proof/growth/2026-09-12-d15-delta/README.md`, `fast-cdp-execution.log`, 3 screenshots at 1680×1050). Remaining, and why this is not verified: development browser only, with no production minification build, no installed desktop package run, and no tablet capture; geometry delta uses element ID and parameter diffing rather than raster pixel-diff shaders or 3D mesh boolean intersection; and historical issues and snapshots are persisted in client project state / LocalStorage rather than a central cloud-synced revision vault. Review corrections 2026-09-12: legacy geometry now fails closed, missed element/layout changes are detected, and slab/roof detail cards are complete. Machine tests pass; full interaction proof remains pending. Prior annotation-comparison and legacy-fallback claims are superseded by proof/growth/2026-09-12-visualise-review/README.md. Review corrections 2026-09-12: legacy geometry now fails closed, missed element/layout changes are detected, and slab/roof detail cards are complete. Machine tests pass; full interaction proof remains pending. Prior annotation-comparison and legacy-fallback claims are superseded by proof/growth/2026-09-12-visualise-review/README.md.
