 ### Z — Zero-loss delivery, release and business continuity
 
 Benchmark references: [Autodesk Forma construction document management](https://construction.autodesk.com/workflows/construction-document-management/), [IBM Maximo](https://www.ibm.com/products/maximo). These references inform the category; each acceptance requirement is X-Ray's own target.
 
-- [ ] **Z-01 Reproducible build identity** — Proof records commit, dirty diff and artifact hashes. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Release stages freeze the source, count the collected files, record executable and installer digests, inject and verify a build identity and check for source drift after quality assurance (proof/growth/2026-09-09-az5-release/verification.md; LATEST-VERIFIED-BUILD.md). Remaining: builds run from an uncommitted working tree, so a commit and dirty-diff record is not part of the identity.
-- [ ] **Z-02 Desktop install and upgrade** — Upgrade retains real user profile and compatible project data. State: gap (assessed 2026-09-10). No build has been installed over the normal application. Every verified package runs from an isolated profile and the register must not imply an upgrade path was tested.
-- [ ] **Z-03 Offline cold start** — Installed app opens source and design without development services. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). The packaged Windows application starts and completes journeys from an isolated profile without development services, including retained sheets, price books and saved views (SC-11, SC-13 native runs). Remaining: cold start is proven for the packaged executable, not for an installed application.
-- [ ] **Z-04 Web production parity** — Built site completes the same declared workflows as development. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Each release runs the same declared journeys against the built site and the native application, most recently 40 runs and 765 commands across both for build 8e14ac427997. Remaining: parity is asserted for the declared journeys only, with rendering web-only and the native refusal documented.
-- [ ] **Z-05 Dependency and license inventory** — Bundled components carry required notices and versions. State: gap (assessed 2026-09-10). There is no dependency and licence inventory with required notices. A bundled third-party CAD engine and a CC0 asset are recorded individually in their slices, which does not satisfy this row.
-- [ ] **Z-06 Security and tenant isolation** — Adversarial access tests cannot cross project permissions. State: dependency-blocked (assessed 2026-09-10). There are no tenants or permissions to cross. Provider keys are confined to server modules (X-10), but adversarial access testing has no boundary to attack until accounts exist.
-- [ ] **Z-07 Backup/restore disaster drill** — Clean machine restoration proves complete recoverability. State: partial (reviewed 2026-09-11). The previously missing apply path now restores transferred backup data into fresh browser storage and verifies the original source SHA-256. Code: src/studio/workspaceRestoreStorage.ts. Proof: proof/growth/2026-09-11-restore/README.md. Remaining: clean-machine and native disaster drills with complete project context; a fresh browser context is not a clean machine.
-- [ ] **Z-08 Observability without secrets** — Error reports omit keys, private prompts and unauthorized documents. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Keys are read only in server modules and never appear in responses, failures or browser code, and evidence bundles are reviewed before archiving (`src/lib/pricingResearch.server.ts`). Remaining: there is no error reporting pipeline to audit for private prompts or unauthorised documents.
-- [ ] **Z-09 Performance and resource limits** — Oversized files fail safely without freezing or partial writes. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Oversized and malformed inputs fail safely rather than corrupting state: workbook import is bounded against zip expansion, backup capture is capped, the CAD bridge enforces a size limit with cancellation, and a failed write preserves the previously stored bytes. Remaining: no freeze or partial-write behaviour has been measured under a deliberate resource-exhaustion test.
-- [ ] **Z-10 Rollback and forward compatibility** — Failed upgrade can recover last confirmed data revision. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). A newer stored revision is never silently overwritten and an unsupported schema fails closed with the original bytes intact, so the last confirmed data revision survives a refused change (`src/studio/persistence.ts`, `store.ts`). Remaining: there is no application upgrade path, so upgrade rollback is untested.
-- [ ] **Z-11 Support and incident handling** — Reproducible ticket links diagnostics, affected build and recovery. State: gap (assessed 2026-09-10). There is no incident or ticket handling linking diagnostics to an affected build.
-- [ ] **Z-12 Proof expiry after changes** — Dependency changes reopen affected completed acceptance rows. State: verified (assessed 2026-09-10). Stated as a governing rule of this register and applied in practice: a dependency change reopens its acceptance, superseded builds are marked superseded rather than deleted, and on 2026-09-10 every row was re-assessed against shipping code with stale claims corrected. Earlier candidate builds remain recorded as superseded with their failures preserved.
-- [ ] **Z-13 Release notes and capability contract** — Users see delivered limits and verified platform support. State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). The verified build record states what the build delivers, its digests, and its known limits including the eight-step assistant pause, web-only rendering and the untested two-window native case (LATEST-VERIFIED-BUILD.md). Remaining: this is a repository record rather than release notes visible to a user inside the application.
