.</p><small class="muted">State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Release stages freeze the source, count the collected files, record executable and installer digests, inject and verify a build identity and check for source drift after quality assurance (proof/growth/2026-09-09-az5-release/verification.md; LATEST-VERIFIED-BUILD.md). Remaining: builds run from an uncommitted working tree, so a commit and dirty-diff record is not part of the identity. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">partial</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-02 desktop install and upgrade upgrade retains real user profile and compatible project data"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-02</div><div><strong>Desktop install and upgrade</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Upgrade retains real user profile and compatible project data.</p><small class="muted">State: gap (assessed 2026-09-10). No build has been installed over the normal application. Every verified package runs from an isolated profile and the register must not imply an upgrade path was tested. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">gap</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-03 offline cold start installed app opens source and design without development services"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-03</div><div><strong>Offline cold start</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Installed app opens source and design without development services.</p><small class="muted">State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). The packaged Windows application starts and completes journeys from an isolated profile without development services, including retained sheets, price books and saved views (SC-11, SC-13 native runs). Remaining: cold start is proven for the packaged executable, not for an installed application. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">partial</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-04 web production parity built site completes the same declared workflows as development"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-04</div><div><strong>Web production parity</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Built site completes the same declared workflows as development.</p><small class="muted">State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Each release runs the same declared journeys against the built site and the native application, most recently 40 runs and 765 commands across both for build 8e14ac427997. Remaining: parity is asserted for the declared journeys only, with rendering web-only and the native refusal documented. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">partial</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-05 dependency and license inventory bundled components carry required notices and versions"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-05</div><div><strong>Dependency and license inventory</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Bundled components carry required notices and versions.</p><small class="muted">State: gap (assessed 2026-09-10). There is no dependency and licence inventory with required notices. A bundled third-party CAD engine and a CC0 asset are recorded individually in their slices, which does not satisfy this row. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">gap</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-06 security and tenant isolation adversarial access tests cannot cross project permissions"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-06</div><div><strong>Security and tenant isolation</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Adversarial access tests cannot cross project permissions.</p><small class="muted">State: dependency-blocked (assessed 2026-09-10). There are no tenants or permissions to cross. Provider keys are confined to server modules (X-10), but adversarial access testing has no boundary to attack until accounts exist. — moved to POST-V1-BACKLOG (v1 scope freeze 2026-09-24).</small></div><span class="state">dependency-blocked</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-07 backup/restore disaster drill clean machine restoration proves complete recoverability"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-07</div><div><strong>Backup/restore disaster drill</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Clean machine restoration proves complete recoverability.</p><small class="muted">State: partial (reviewed 2026-09-11). The previously missing apply path now restores transferred backup data into fresh browser storage and verifies the original source SHA-256. Code: src/studio/workspaceRestoreStorage.ts. Proof: proof/growth/2026-09-11-restore/README.md. Remaining: clean-machine and native disaster drills with complete project context; a fresh browser context is not a clean machine. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">partial</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-08 observability without secrets error reports omit keys, private prompts and unauthorized documents"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-08</div><div><strong>Observability without secrets</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Error reports omit keys, private prompts and unauthorized documents.</p><small class="muted">State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Keys are read only in server modules and never appear in responses, failures or browser code, and evidence bundles are reviewed before archiving (`src/lib/pricingResearch.server.ts`). Remaining: there is no error reporting pipeline to audit for private prompts or unauthorised documents. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">partial</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-09 performance and resource limits oversized files fail safely without freezing or partial writes"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-09</div><div><strong>Performance and resource limits</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Oversized files fail safely without freezing or partial writes.</p><small class="muted">State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). Oversized and malformed inputs fail safely rather than corrupting state: workbook import is bounded against zip expansion, backup capture is capped, the CAD bridge enforces a size limit with cancellation, and a failed write preserves the previously stored bytes. Remaining: no freeze or partial-write behaviour has been measured under a deliberate resource-exhaustion test. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">partial</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-10 rollback and forward compatibility failed upgrade can recover last confirmed data revision"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-10</div><div><strong>Rollback and forward compatibility</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Failed upgrade can recover last confirmed data revision.</p><small class="muted">State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). A newer stored revision is never silently overwritten and an unsupported schema fails closed with the original bytes intact, so the last confirmed data revision survives a refused change (`src/studio/persistence.ts`, `store.ts`). Remaining: there is no application upgrade path, so upgrade rollback is untested. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">partial</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-11 support and incident handling reproducible ticket links diagnostics, affected build and recovery"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-11</div><div><strong>Support and incident handling</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Reproducible ticket links diagnostics, affected build and recovery.</p><small class="muted">State: gap (assessed 2026-09-10). There is no incident or ticket handling linking diagnostics to an affected build. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">gap</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-12 proof expiry after changes dependency changes reopen affected completed acceptance rows"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-12</div><div><strong>Proof expiry after changes</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Dependency changes reopen affected completed acceptance rows.</p><small class="muted">State: verified (assessed 2026-09-10). Stated as a governing rule of this register and applied in practice: a dependency change reopens its acceptance, superseded builds are marked superseded rather than deleted, and on 2026-09-10 every row was re-assessed against shipping code with stale claims corrected. Earlier candidate builds remain recorded as superseded with their failures preserved. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">verified</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-13 release notes and capability contract users see delivered limits and verified platform support"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-13</div><div><strong>Release notes and capability contract</strong><p class="acceptance">Pass when: Users see delivered limits and verified platform support.</p><small class="muted">State: partial (assessed 2026-09-10). The verified build record states what the build delivers, its digests, and its known limits including the eight-step assistant pause, web-only rendering and the untested two-window native case (LATEST-VERIFIED-BUILD.md). Remaining: this is a repository record rather than release notes visible to a user inside the application. [section 06]</small></div><span class="state">partial</span></article><article class="requirement" data-search="z-14 full working-day acceptance named industry scenario completes create-to-handover-to-reopen with proof"><div class="reqid"><span class="check" aria-label="Unchecked"></span>Z-14</div><div><strong>Full working